This is the coding agent's whole `Program.cs`. It starts the terminal host with the coding agent's catalogue. To start your own agent, you would write your own catalogue in its place.

```csharp
using LouisAgent.Core.Web;
using LouisAgent.Core.Anthropic;
using LouisAgent.Core.Ollama;
using LouisAgent.Core.OpenAi;
using LouisAgent.Core.Telemetry;
using LouisAgent.Core.Hosting;
using LouisAgent.Cli;
using LouisAgent.Coding;

// Usage: LouisAgent.CodingCli [prompt]. It loads what this demo's catalogue names.
await CliHost.RunAsync(args, CodingCatalog.Create(), new HostServices(WebAccess.Create, OtlpTelemetry.Instance, Providers: [AnthropicProvider.Instance, OllamaProvider.Instance, OpenAiCompatibleProvider.Instance]));
```

The call at the bottom does the work. `CliHost.RunAsync` builds the agent, then runs it in the terminal. `CodingCatalog.Create()` names the identities and packs it loads. `HostServices` lends it web access, telemetry and the model providers.

Its settings go in files in the demo's `config` folder. Put your key in the secrets file:

```bash
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your-key-here
```

To pick a local model instead, set the profile in your shell before the run:

```bash
LLM_PROFILE=ollama
```

Then, from `demos/louis-agent/hosts/LouisAgent.CodingCli`, run `dotnet run`.

The page's listing is the coding agent's own program. Your agent gets its own catalogue, with your identities and packs. The doc below says how the host runs it.

